course: arts
lesson: 10
title: Instruments of the world: bandura, erhu, sitar, kora, didgeridoo and more
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every corner of the world built its own instruments from what was at hand: gourds, termite-hollowed branches, bamboo, old oil drums, and they carry the stories of the people who play them.
explain: Picture a world music fair: a Ukrainian bandura with dozens of strings, a Chinese erhu with only two, an Indian sitar with strings nobody touches that still hum, a West African kora that is half harp, half lute, and a didgeridoo droning without a pause for breath.
explain: The trick is not to squeeze new things into old boxes: the kora is not "a guitar or a harp", the guzheng is not "a sideways piano", and bagpipes are not only Scottish.
explain: Fewer strings does not mean easier, drums can change their pitch, and a whole continent like Africa, with 54 countries, is never "just drums".
explain: People say it their own way: in Ireland "yer man" means that fella, "so they are" adds force in Northern Ireland, Gen Z "lowkey" means kind of, and Londoners say "bruv" for mate.
fact: The bandura is a Ukrainian plucked string instrument; modern concert banduras have around 55 to 65 strings.
fact: The bandura was played by kobzars, wandering singers of stories and songs; historians have documented that many kobzars were persecuted in the Soviet Union in the 1930s.
fact: The erhu is a Chinese bowed instrument with two strings; the bow hair passes between the strings, and it can sound very like a human voice.
fact: The sitar has sympathetic strings that are not plucked but vibrate by themselves when matching notes are played; Ravi Shankar made it famous worldwide, and George Harrison of the Beatles learned from him.
fact: The kora is a West African instrument with 21 strings, made from a large gourd covered in skin, often called a harp-lute, and traditionally played by griots, who keep history through song.
fact: The didgeridoo comes from Aboriginal Australians of northern Australia, traditionally made from a branch hollowed by termites; skilled players use circular breathing.
fact: Circular breathing means breathing in through the nose while pushing out air stored in the cheeks, so the sound never stops.
fact: Many places have bagpipes: Scotland, Ireland's uilleann pipes, the gaita of Galicia in Spain and the gaida of Bulgaria.
fact: The tabla is a pair of Indian hand drums; pressing the skin while striking it changes the pitch.
fact: The steel pan was developed in Trinidad and Tobago in the 20th century from oil drums and is its national instrument.
fact: The guzheng is a Chinese plucked zither with strings over movable bridges, played in China for over 2,000 years.
fact: African music includes drums such as the djembe, string instruments like the kora, thumb pianos like the mbira of southern Africa, and many singing styles.
fact: The trembita is a long wooden horn of the Hutsuls in the Ukrainian Carpathians, often around 3 metres or more, used for signals in the mountains and at ceremonies; the Swiss alphorn is a similar mountain horn.
exercise: Conor laughs at a bandura with fifty-odd strings. What is it? | A Ukrainian plucked string instrument with many strings | A harp that someone renamed just for fun, with a few strings added | A broken piano without its keys | A guitar with extra strings glued on
exercise: Who traditionally played the bandura? | Kobzars, wandering singers of stories | Griots, the singers and storytellers of West Africa | Drummers in Trinidad, who play steel pans | Cathedral choirboys from English churches
exercise: Zak thinks a two-string erhu must be simple. What is true? | It takes great skill and can sound like a voice | It only plays two notes, one on each of its strings | It is just a toy for babies and toddlers in Beijing | It is played by hitting it with two wooden sticks
exercise: Aarav's sitar has strings nobody touches. What do they do? | Vibrate by themselves and make the shimmering sound | Nothing at all, they are spare strings kept in case one of the others breaks | They are only for decoration on the neck | They hold the instrument together
exercise: Which Beatle learned the sitar from Ravi Shankar? | George Harrison | Michael Jackson | Freddie Mercury | Mick Jagger
exercise: Jordan asks if the kora is a guitar or a harp. What is it? | Its own type, often called a harp-lute | Just a guitar with a big round gourd stuck on the back | Just a harp turned on its side | A kind of drum with strings for show
exercise: How many strings does a kora have? | 21 | 2 | 6 | 100
exercise: How does a didgeridoo player keep going for ages? | Circular breathing: in through the nose while blowing out | Holding one enormous breath for ten whole minutes or even more | A hidden air tank in the didgeridoo | Taking turns with a second player
exercise: Sean says bagpipes are only Scottish. What is true? | Many places have bagpipes, like Ireland's uilleann pipes | Bagpipes exist only in Scotland and nowhere else in the world | Bagpipes were invented last century in Glasgow | Bagpipes are an Irish drum
exercise: Riya's tabla "talk". How? | Pressing the skin while striking changes the pitch | Tabla have tiny speakers hidden inside the skins that play voices | Drums can only make one sound | The player hums along to fool you
exercise: Malik's steel pan is made from oil drums. What is true? | It was developed in Trinidad and Tobago and is its national instrument | Real instruments can never be made from old oil drums, only from good wood | It was invented in Toronto last year | It is a woodwind made of tin
exercise: Ava calls the guzheng a sideways piano. What is it? | A Chinese plucked zither, played for over 2,000 years | A piano that somebody knocked over onto its side | An electric keyboard with strings | A drum with a wooden lid
exercise: Ethan says African music is just drums. What is true? | Africa has 54 countries and many kinds of music | All African music is drumming, in every one of its countries | Africa has only one style of music | Only West Africa has any music
exercise: What is an mbira? | A thumb piano from southern Africa | A Ukrainian mountain horn made of wood | A Chinese fiddle with only two strings | A Scottish bagpipe with three drones
exercise: Ostap's cousin says only Switzerland has long horns. What is true? | The Hutsuls in Ukraine have the trembita | Only the Swiss make long horns, everyone else copies them | The trembita is a Swiss copy | Long horns don't exist outside films
exercise: What was the trembita used for? | Signals in the mountains and ceremonies | Calling taxis in the city and in the villages | Playing pop songs on the radio at weddings | Stirring big pots of soup at village feasts
test: Your mate goes, "more strings = harder, so the erhu's dead easy." What is wrong? | Fewer strings does not mean easier; the erhu takes great skill | he's right, two strings is simple, anyone could pick it up in a day | The erhu actually has 50 strings | The erhu isn't played at all
test: Conor in Dublin says, "Yer man's having a laugh" ("that fella's joking") about the bandura's strings. What should he learn? | It really has around 55 to 65 strings and a long history | It has two real strings, and the rest are fake ones just for show | It is just an Irish harp with a new name | It is a joke instrument from a TV show
test: What would happen if you cut off all the sympathetic strings on a sitar? | It would lose much of its shimmering sound | It would sound exactly the same, because those strings are never touched | It would get much louder | It would stop working completely
test: A cartoon shows a didgeridoo player turning blue holding his breath. What's wrong? | Players breathe in through the nose while blowing out | Nothing, players really hold their breath | Didgeridoos are played by hitting them | Players breathe through the didgeridoo backwards
test: Sean in Belfast says, "Bagpipes are Scottish, so they are." Which fact puts it right? | Ireland, Spain and Bulgaria have their own bagpipes | Bagpipes come only from England, and the Scots borrowed them | No country has bagpipes any more | Bagpipes are a kind of trumpet
test: Ethan says, "African music is just drums, everyone knows." Which instrument shows he is wrong? | The kora, a 21-string harp-lute | The djembe, a West African hand drum | The snare drum, a marching band drum | The bass drum, an orchestra drum
test: Someone wants to play a didgeridoo at a festival. What is respectful? | Learn about and respect the culture it comes from | Ignore where it comes from completely | Pretend it was invented in their own town to make it more fun | Paint over the traditional designs
test: Malik's friend says, "No way, oil drums can't be real instruments, eh." What is the truth? | Great instruments can be made from what is at hand | Real instruments must always come from proper factories | Oil drums can only make noise, not notes | Steel pans are banned in Canada
test: Zak calls the erhu "lowkey wild" (kind of wild). What makes it unusual? | The bow hair passes between its two strings | It has no strings at all, only a bow | It is played with the feet | It is made of ice
test: Tom says, "Drums can only make one sound, bruv." What does the tabla show? | Drums can change pitch and make many sounds | He's right, every drum has one sound | Tabla are not really drums | Only electric drums can change sound
test: Ava calls the guzheng a sideways piano. What would show her it isn't? | It is plucked with the fingers, not played with keys | It has keys hidden underneath where you can't see them | It is played with a bow | It is blown like a horn
exam: Identify the principal means by which a player of the didgeridoo sustains an uninterrupted sound. | Circular breathing, inhaling through the nose while expelling air stored in the cheeks | The retention of a single breath for several minutes | A concealed reservoir of air within the instrument | The alternation of two players upon one instrument
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sympathetic strings of the sitar. | They vibrate without being plucked when corresponding notes are played | They are reserve strings for use upon breakage | They serve a purely decorative purpose | They secure the structure of the instrument
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the geographical distribution of bagpipes? | Bagpipes are found in many lands, including Ireland, Galicia and Bulgaria | Bagpipes are confined exclusively to Scotland and have never been played elsewhere | Bagpipes are unknown outside the British Isles | Bagpipes were devised in the twentieth century
exam: Ascertain the origin of the steel pan. | It was developed in Trinidad and Tobago in the twentieth century from oil drums | It was devised in a European conservatoire of the eighteenth century | It was manufactured first in Canada in recent years | It derives from the long horns of the Carpathian mountains
